notes.text:
namaste greets and takes leave at any hour and with anyone. German:
- changes the formula with the time of day (Guten Morgen, Guten Abend) and with du / Sie;
- has a separate goodbye (Tschüss, Auf Wiedersehen).

Hei / Moi (also used for goodbye) and Hyvää huomenta are like Hallo and Guten Morgen.

- German keeps hello and goodbye apart (Hallo – Tschüss);
- the du / Sie choice is made more often than sinä / te.

Oli otya? – Bulungi works like Wie geht’s? – Gut. German adds:
- the choice du / Sie (Wie geht es dir? / Ihnen?);
- a formula for each part of the day.

If your language greets differently by time of day and by familiarity, the system is familiar:
- Guten Morgen / Tag / Abend, Gute Nacht only for parting;
- du-people get Hallo and Tschüss, Sie-people Guten Tag and Auf Wiedersehen.

German greetings are fixed formulas of separate words, chosen by the time of day and by du or Sie:
- Guten Morgen, Guten Tag, Guten Abend;
- Hallo – Tschüss for friends, Guten Tag – Auf Wiedersehen for others.
